# Nova plugin lifecycle

Read [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md) for ownership, command semantics and release
authorization. Use this workflow for build/test/publication validation.

1. Inspect `project.json`, its PowerShell target, manifest GUID and coverage
   paths. Check installed command syntax; the generated guide was verified with
   NovaModuleTools 3.4.1. Do not invent a project-validation or package cmdlet.
2. From the project root run `./scripts/Test-PluginBoundary.ps1`, then
   `Get-NovaProjectInfo`, `Invoke-NovaBuild`,
   `Invoke-NovaTest`, then `Test-NovaBuild`. Record assertions and coverage
   separately; a narrow tag selection still enforces project-wide coverage.
3. Run the analyzer commands in AGENTS.md and fix findings. Do not add
   suppressions or skip tests to manufacture success.
4. If local installation is authorized, run in a separate process:
   `Publish-NovaModule -Local -ModuleDirectoryPath '/path/to/test-owned/modules'`.
   Keep Gerber configuration isolated, expose the module root to a fresh host,
   restart Gerber, invoke `/{{Namespace}} hello` and inspect `/plugins list`.
   The GUID must match; known metadata alone must never imply loaded/installed.
5. For version/package/release requests only, inspect `Update-NovaModuleVersion`,
   `New-NovaModulePackage` or `Invoke-NovaRelease` help and the branch/changelog.
   Use `-WhatIf` first and obtain explicit authorization for mutation. Do not
   commit, tag, push or remotely publish as an implicit validation step.
